; The tier that matters most: an FFI program that is STILL freestanding.
;
; `rust/examples/nostd` is `#![no_std]` and its `alloc` is wired to
; `axiom_alloc`. The six memory intrinsics the precompiled sysroot
; `alloc` rlib would otherwise import - memcpy, memmove, memset,
; memcmp, bzero, strlen - come from `axiom-ffi`'s `nostd-runtime`
; feature (`nostd_runtime.rs`), not from the example crate. Measured,
; an Axiom executable linked against it has an EMPTY `nm -u` - the same
; answer a program with no `extern` at all gives.
;
; Its `axiom-allow.txt` therefore permits NOTHING, and an empty
; allowlist is the strongest statement `check-ffi.sh` can make: the FFI
; is in use and `MM-FFI-1`'s property is intact anyway.
;
; The value is checked against an independent implementation rather
; than merely printed, so a wrong byte-slice layout fails here instead
; of looking like a hash.
; The binding is IMPORTED rather than hand-declared. It used to be
; hand-declared, and the consequence was that `rust/examples/nostd/axiom/Hash.ax`
; - regenerated and diffed by this gate - was never compiled into a
; program: `repeatByte`, the only wrapper that reaches Rust's
; `alloc::vec!` through `axiom_alloc`, was generated, diffed, and never
; run. A generated module with no consumer is a golden, not a binding.
(import IO)

(import Str)

(import Hash)

;@axiom:effect(io)
(pub fn (main)
  (if (!= (fnv1a "axiom") 7929549642058804759)
    1
    ; `repeatByte` allocates in Rust's `alloc` on Axiom's arena and
    ; hands the bytes back through the out-cell protocol.
    (if (strEq (repeatByte 65 3) "AAA")
      {
        (println "nostd: agree")
        0
      }
      2
    )
  )
)
; expect 0
